WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.150 --> 00:00:01.692
PRIYANKA VERGADIA:
If you are looking

00:00:01.692 --> 00:00:04.380
to simplify the development
of complicated conversational

00:00:04.380 --> 00:00:07.350
experiences, then you've
clicked on the right video.

00:00:07.350 --> 00:00:09.266
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:14.060 --> 00:00:16.450
I'm Priyanka Vergadia,
and in this episode

00:00:16.450 --> 00:00:19.120
of "Deconstructing Chat
Bots," I will share with you

00:00:19.120 --> 00:00:22.060
how Dialogflow CX
simplifies and accelerates

00:00:22.060 --> 00:00:26.440
the creation of enterprise-level
conversational experiences.

00:00:26.440 --> 00:00:30.520
Let's say PUggs is a huge
e-commerce company that sells

00:00:30.520 --> 00:00:32.530
shoes for our furry friends.

00:00:32.530 --> 00:00:35.110
Customer service is
critical for their business,

00:00:35.110 --> 00:00:38.320
obviously, because furry
friends bark sometimes.

00:00:38.320 --> 00:00:41.200
When they started three years
ago, the company was small.

00:00:41.200 --> 00:00:42.850
Their product
options were limited,

00:00:42.850 --> 00:00:44.440
and their customer
service did not

00:00:44.440 --> 00:00:46.300
require too many
complicated twists

00:00:46.300 --> 00:00:48.160
and turns in the conversation.

00:00:48.160 --> 00:00:50.920
And the one developer
and Dialogflow essentials

00:00:50.920 --> 00:00:53.440
was enough to handle
the demand of a fairly

00:00:53.440 --> 00:00:55.300
simple conversational flow.

00:00:55.300 --> 00:00:59.020
But this year, their business
has grown exponentially,

00:00:59.020 --> 00:01:01.600
with more products,
customers, and a bigger

00:01:01.600 --> 00:01:04.750
team, which has led to an
evolution of their customer

00:01:04.750 --> 00:01:05.710
service.

00:01:05.710 --> 00:01:08.500
Their customers now have
complex conversations,

00:01:08.500 --> 00:01:10.660
which drove them
towards a platform that

00:01:10.660 --> 00:01:12.790
can simplify the
process of developing

00:01:12.790 --> 00:01:14.530
a conversational experience.

00:01:14.530 --> 00:01:17.560
That's where
Dialogflow CX comes in.

00:01:17.560 --> 00:01:21.140
Dialogflow CX makes it easy
for not just the developers

00:01:21.140 --> 00:01:24.280
but also the conversational
designers and contact center

00:01:24.280 --> 00:01:27.610
managers to build the
conversational flows.

00:01:27.610 --> 00:01:31.270
PUggs team is excited
because with Dialogflow CX,

00:01:31.270 --> 00:01:33.400
they have the visual
flow vendor, which

00:01:33.400 --> 00:01:36.250
makes it easy to understand,
navigate, and edit

00:01:36.250 --> 00:01:37.720
complex flows.

00:01:37.720 --> 00:01:40.700
It is optimized for large
enterprise use cases,

00:01:40.700 --> 00:01:42.520
allowing multiple
different flows

00:01:42.520 --> 00:01:44.830
within a single virtual agent.

00:01:44.830 --> 00:01:46.720
Think of flows as
a reuseable way

00:01:46.720 --> 00:01:49.660
to contain and control
a specific part

00:01:49.660 --> 00:01:53.530
of the conversation, such as
user authentication or payment

00:01:53.530 --> 00:01:54.490
processing.

00:01:54.490 --> 00:01:57.160
If you're familiar with
mega agents and subagent

00:01:57.160 --> 00:01:59.350
concept in Diaglogflow
essentials,

00:01:59.350 --> 00:02:01.540
flows serve a very
similar purpose.

00:02:01.540 --> 00:02:04.520
They can be reused
anywhere in the agent.

00:02:04.520 --> 00:02:08.830
So if PUggs has different teams
managing orders and returns,

00:02:08.830 --> 00:02:11.440
they can effectively
work together

00:02:11.440 --> 00:02:14.320
by calling each other's
flows while having

00:02:14.320 --> 00:02:16.360
full control of their own flow.

00:02:16.360 --> 00:02:19.300
They even have versioning
and environment capabilities

00:02:19.300 --> 00:02:21.940
at the flow level, which
facilitates each team

00:02:21.940 --> 00:02:25.220
to work on their flows
at their own pace.

00:02:25.220 --> 00:02:28.060
The state-based data
model in Dialogflow CX

00:02:28.060 --> 00:02:31.660
allows them to easily
reuse intents, entities,

00:02:31.660 --> 00:02:32.680
and webhooks.

00:02:32.680 --> 00:02:36.100
They can also intuitively
define transitions and handle

00:02:36.100 --> 00:02:38.650
deviations from the main topic.

00:02:38.650 --> 00:02:40.360
With the native
IVR settings, they

00:02:40.360 --> 00:02:43.360
can easily convert
an agent into an IVR

00:02:43.360 --> 00:02:45.790
and integrate with their
telephony partners.

00:02:45.790 --> 00:02:48.280
And access to an advanced
analytics dashboard

00:02:48.280 --> 00:02:51.160
provides easy assessment
of the model performance

00:02:51.160 --> 00:02:53.590
to better serve their users.

00:02:53.590 --> 00:02:55.910
Now that we have a
basic understanding,

00:02:55.910 --> 00:02:59.320
let's take a little tour of
the Dialogflow CS console.

00:02:59.320 --> 00:03:02.680
Once we are in, we see
the project and the agent

00:03:02.680 --> 00:03:04.010
that we are working with.

00:03:04.010 --> 00:03:07.720
We can also have multiple
agents within one project.

00:03:07.720 --> 00:03:11.320
The graph in the center
shows a visual representation

00:03:11.320 --> 00:03:13.450
of the selected
conversational flow,

00:03:13.450 --> 00:03:16.120
similar to a
state-machine diagram.

00:03:16.120 --> 00:03:18.370
Using this Graph
Setting button, we

00:03:18.370 --> 00:03:20.800
can zoom in and out
of the flow and change

00:03:20.800 --> 00:03:22.420
orientation as needed.

00:03:22.420 --> 00:03:25.360
I have this very simple
agent that has one flow.

00:03:25.360 --> 00:03:27.650
We see all the transitions here.

00:03:27.650 --> 00:03:29.200
Then I have this
other agent that

00:03:29.200 --> 00:03:33.340
has multiple flows branching
out from the default start flow.

00:03:33.340 --> 00:03:37.610
If I select one of these flows,
I see the graph for that flow.

00:03:37.610 --> 00:03:40.120
The first node of the
graph, called Start,

00:03:40.120 --> 00:03:41.740
is the Start page of the flow.

00:03:41.740 --> 00:03:44.320
And these connecting lines
from one node to another

00:03:44.320 --> 00:03:46.630
represent possible transitions.

00:03:46.630 --> 00:03:49.180
Each node of the graph
represents a page.

00:03:49.180 --> 00:03:52.240
We can click a node to
expand it and edit the data.

00:03:52.240 --> 00:03:54.370
Some content-specific
panels open

00:03:54.370 --> 00:03:56.770
on the right side of the
console to give more room

00:03:56.770 --> 00:03:58.480
to fill the details.

00:03:58.480 --> 00:04:01.930
The Build tab on the far left
contains the sections to manage

00:04:01.930 --> 00:04:04.070
the flows and the pages.

00:04:04.070 --> 00:04:06.280
When you select
a flow, the pages

00:04:06.280 --> 00:04:10.450
contained within that flow are
listed in the Pages section.

00:04:10.450 --> 00:04:13.390
The Manage tab contains
resources and tools

00:04:13.390 --> 00:04:15.910
available to the
agent, such as intents,

00:04:15.910 --> 00:04:20.360
entity types, webhooks,
deployment options, and more.

00:04:20.360 --> 00:04:22.210
We will look at all
of this in detail

00:04:22.210 --> 00:04:23.680
in the upcoming episodes.

00:04:23.680 --> 00:04:27.430
This indicator shows the status
of the tasks like training.

00:04:27.430 --> 00:04:30.850
The Agent Settings button
opens the Agent Settings panel

00:04:30.850 --> 00:04:34.420
where we can modify the agent
name, machine learning, IVR

00:04:34.420 --> 00:04:38.890
settings, languages we want to
support, and more such things.

00:04:38.890 --> 00:04:41.510
And on the far right
we have the simulator,

00:04:41.510 --> 00:04:44.920
which helps us test our
agent as we are designing it.

00:04:44.920 --> 00:04:47.740
Notice that here we can
choose the environment we want

00:04:47.740 --> 00:04:50.710
to test in, and it even
selects the specific flow we

00:04:50.710 --> 00:04:53.920
are testing, especially
useful for PUggs

00:04:53.920 --> 00:04:56.770
where different teams
manage each flow.

00:04:56.770 --> 00:04:59.350
All right, so that was
a quick introduction

00:04:59.350 --> 00:05:02.560
of Dialogflow CX with a
little tour of the console.

00:05:02.560 --> 00:05:05.290
We saw how it helps
simplify the development

00:05:05.290 --> 00:05:08.050
of complicated
conversational experiences.

00:05:08.050 --> 00:05:10.060
Join me in the
next episode where

00:05:10.060 --> 00:05:13.750
we will follow PUggs' journey to
dive deeper into the Dialogflow

00:05:13.750 --> 00:05:17.560
CX console and to understand
the main components-- the flows,

00:05:17.560 --> 00:05:19.360
the pages, and transitions.

00:05:19.360 --> 00:05:22.030
Until then, comment
below to share with me

00:05:22.030 --> 00:05:24.160
your Conversational AI use case.

00:05:24.160 --> 00:05:26.910
[MUSIC PLAYING]

